  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions\n\nThe morning convective complex has largely dissipated as of 1 PM\nwith a few lingering showers remaining. The weak, slow-moving\nshort wave trough that helped drive this system is centered \nover central KS with a slow southeastward progression, and a \ngenerally lessening influence this far north. The morning storms\ndeveloped in a rather moist airmass, with low level moisture \nfeed off the Gulf and a mid/upper level moisture feed around the\nridge from the Baja region. This helped the stronger cells to \nproduce 2-3\" per hour rainfall rates where they stalled. This \natmospheric moisture content is actually expected to increase \nslightly in the next 36 hours with the approach of a stronger \nshort wave trough out of the CA/NV region. This should bring \nPWAT of 2-2.2\" to parts of the area in advance of the next round\nof storms. The incoming western short wave trough will dampen \nthe upper ridge and cool temperatures a bit over the next few \ndays, in addition to precip potential. \n\nThrough Early Thursday\n\nSome weak smaller-scale forcing will rotate around the ridge\ntonight with a slight increase in low level jet speed over\ncentral KS. This will increase moisture transport into western\nparts of the forecast area with some LLJ convergence gradually\nlifting northeast into the forecast area around sunrise or\nshortly thereafter. This should support another round of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms with potential for pockets\nof heavy rain. This area of weak forcing may persist into the\nearly afternoon hours, with the main question being how\nwidespread any convective showers/storms can be. Deep forcing is\nweak, but capping is very limited and when paired with a deep\nmoist environment it probably won't take much forcing to sustain\nscattered storms. If convection is more widespread tomorrow, it\nmay limit daytime heating and instability recovery which would\nlessen the late day severe potential...but fewer storms during\nthe day may warrant a bit more of a damaging wind potential for\nlate Thursday.\n\nLate Thursday into Friday\n\nThe main show is expected on Thursday evening as the stronger \ndeep layer forcing pushes east. Expect the cold front entering \nnorth central Nebraska to convect along the boundary. This will\nlikely transition quickly into an MCS as numerous updrafts in \nclose proximity conglomerate into a single forward propagating \ncold pool. The theta-e and MLCAPE axes will extend from that \nnorth central NE convection to the southeast while low level \nshear vectors point east or east southeasterly. This would \nappear to favor individual outflow surges directed toward the \neast southeast while the larger MCS outflow system eats toward \nthe southeast into the instability axis. That instability does \nnot extend terribly far east of the Missouri River so the \nintensity should wane with eastward progression. The wind and \ninstability fields with this system appear to be sufficient to \nsupport severe wind in segments, but are not forecast to be \nimpressive enough to support a persistent high-end wind storm \nacross a wide expanse. Additionally, the moisture content \nsuggests that rainfall rates could exceed 2\" per hour in heavier\ncores, so any training elements will need to be monitored for \nflooding potential. Showers or a storm may linger into Friday, \nbut the trough passes east of the area with subsidence \novertaking and drying out the region by late in the day. \n\nSaturday Into Next Week\nA rather weak northeastward extension of the upper ridge axis\nfrom the Four Corners region will keep the local area mainly dry\nthrough Monday or so, with surface high pressure slowly drifting\noverhead and then east of the area. Highs will be largely in the\n80s to low 90s with lows in the 60s in a less humid airmass. By\nlate Monday into Tuesday, model guidance is strong in bringing a\nglancing influence of a strong Canadian trough with a front\nsagging toward northern parts of the area. This will bring\nthunderstorm chances if the front makes it this far south.",
